00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,966
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll learn
the ratio test, perfect for factorials and

2
00:00:05,966 --> 00:00:06,912
exponentials.

3
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:13,644
Find L, the limit of the absolute value of
a sub n plus one over a sub n.

4
00:00:14,094 --> 00:00:19,876
If L is less than one, the series
converges absolutely. If L is greater than

5
00:00:19,876 --> 00:00:24,441
one, it diverges. If L equals one, the
test is inconclusive.

6
00:00:25,567 --> 00:00:31,817
The sum of two to the n over n factorial.
The ratio is two over n plus one, which

7
00:00:31,817 --> 00:00:32,820
goes to zero.

8
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:35,851
Zero is less than one, so it converges.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,212
The sum of n squared over three to the n.
The ratio is n plus one over n, squared,

10
00:00:43,212 --> 00:00:44,424
times one third.

11
00:00:44,874 --> 00:00:47,370
That goes to one third, so it converges.

12
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:51,188
For any p-series, the ratio goes to one.

13
00:00:51,638 --> 00:00:55,926
So the ratio test can't decide them; use
the p rule instead.

14
00:00:57,067 --> 00:01:03,620
Factorials or exponentials: ratio test.
Powers of n: p-series or comparison.

15
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:07,328
Alternating signs: alternating series
test.

16
00:01:07,778 --> 00:01:10,466
Always start with the nth term test.

17
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:16,976
Your turn. Use the ratio test on the sum
of n factorial over five to the n.

18
00:01:17,426 --> 00:01:18,962
Pause the video and try it.

19
00:01:19,412 --> 00:01:24,895
The ratio is n plus one over five, which
goes to infinity. It diverges.

20
00:01:26,033 --> 00:01:31,792
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll see that
some series converge more robustly than

21
00:01:31,792 --> 00:01:32,284
others.

22
00:01:33,433 --> 00:01:38,830
A series converges absolutely if the
series of absolute values converges.

23
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:42,054
Absolute convergence implies convergence.

24
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:48,716
A series converges conditionally if it
converges, but the series of absolute

25
00:01:48,716 --> 00:01:49,877
values diverges.

26
00:01:50,327 --> 00:01:53,932
The alternating harmonic series is the
classic example.

27
00:01:55,066 --> 00:02:00,247
To classify, test the absolute values
first. If they converge, you're done:

28
00:02:00,247 --> 00:02:00,869
absolute.

29
00:02:01,319 --> 00:02:06,460
If not, test the original series, for
example with the alternating series test.

30
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:12,421
Absolutely convergent series can be
rearranged without changing the sum.

31
00:02:12,871 --> 00:02:17,948
Conditionally convergent ones can be
rearranged to add up to any number at all.

32
00:02:19,066 --> 00:02:24,037
The sum of negative one to the n over n
squared converges absolutely.

33
00:02:24,487 --> 00:02:29,372
The sum of negative one to the n over root
n converges conditionally.

34
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:36,516
Your turn. Classify the sum of negative
one to the n over n to the one point five.

35
00:02:36,966 --> 00:02:38,502
Pause the video and try it.

36
00:02:38,952 --> 00:02:44,375
The absolute values form a p-series with p
greater than one, so it converges

37
00:02:44,375 --> 00:02:45,160
absolutely.

38
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:51,661
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll build
polynomials that imitate any smooth

39
00:02:51,661 --> 00:02:53,233
function near a point.

40
00:02:54,367 --> 00:02:59,584
The idea: match the function's value,
slope, concavity and higher derivatives at

41
00:02:59,584 --> 00:03:00,105
a point.

42
00:03:00,555 --> 00:03:03,627
The more derivatives match, the better the
fit nearby.

43
00:03:04,767 --> 00:03:12,295
The nth degree Taylor polynomial at a is
the sum from k equals zero to n of f's kth

44
00:03:12,295 --> 00:03:17,737
derivative at a, over k factorial, times x
minus a to the k.

45
00:03:18,187 --> 00:03:21,579
Centered at zero, it's called a Maclaurin
polynomial.

46
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:26,007
For e to the x at zero, every derivative
is one.

47
00:03:26,457 --> 00:03:32,912
P three of x is one plus x plus x squared
over two plus x cubed over six. At x

48
00:03:32,912 --> 00:03:37,465
equals one, that's two point six six
seven, close to e.

49
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:45,171
For sine x at zero: x minus x cubed over
six plus x to the fifth over one twenty.

50
00:03:45,621 --> 00:03:48,202
Only odd powers appear, since sine is odd.

51
00:03:49,333 --> 00:03:52,427
Center at a different point when you need
accuracy there.

52
00:03:52,877 --> 00:03:59,768
For ln x at one: x minus one, minus x
minus one squared over two, plus x minus

53
00:03:59,768 --> 00:04:01,623
one cubed over three.

54
00:04:02,767 --> 00:04:07,524
Your turn. Find the second degree
Maclaurin polynomial for cosine x.

55
00:04:07,974 --> 00:04:09,510
Pause the video and try it.

56
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:12,008
One minus x squared over two.

57
00:04:13,133 --> 00:04:19,058
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll find how
far a Taylor polynomial can be from the

58
00:04:19,058 --> 00:04:19,853
true value.

59
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,629
The remainder is the difference between
the function and its Taylor polynomial.

60
00:04:26,079 --> 00:04:29,343
We can't usually find it exactly, but we
can bound it.

61
00:04:30,466 --> 00:04:36,864
The Lagrange error bound: the error is at
most M over n plus one factorial, times

62
00:04:36,864 --> 00:04:40,813
the absolute value of x minus a to the n
plus one.

63
00:04:41,263 --> 00:04:45,359
M is the biggest value of the next
derivative between a and x.

64
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:52,557
Using P three for e to the one: the fourth
derivative is e to the x, at most three on

65
00:04:52,557 --> 00:04:53,412
zero to one.

66
00:04:53,862 --> 00:04:59,240
The error is at most three over twenty
four, which is point one two five. The

67
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:01,755
true error is about point zero five.

68
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:08,488
For sine of point one using x minus x
cubed over six, every derivative is at

69
00:05:08,488 --> 00:05:09,150
most one.

70
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:14,649
The error is at most point one to the
fifth over one twenty, less than one ten

71
00:05:14,649 --> 00:05:15,296
millionth.

72
00:05:16,433 --> 00:05:22,384
To guarantee an error under point zero
zero one for e to the half, find n with

73
00:05:22,384 --> 00:05:28,717
three times point five to the n plus one
over n plus one factorial below point zero

74
00:05:28,717 --> 00:05:29,404
zero one.

75
00:05:29,854 --> 00:05:31,219
n equals four works.

76
00:05:32,366 --> 00:05:36,996
Your turn. Bound the error of using one
plus x for e to the point two.

77
00:05:37,446 --> 00:05:38,982
Pause the video and try it.

78
00:05:39,432 --> 00:05:46,085
M is at most e to the point two, under one
point three. Error at most one point three

79
00:05:46,085 --> 00:05:50,546
times point zero four over two, about
point zero two six.

80
00:05:51,666 --> 00:05:57,042
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll study
series with x in them: power series.

81
00:05:58,166 --> 00:06:02,753
A power series is the sum of c sub n times
x minus a to the n.

82
00:06:03,203 --> 00:06:08,195
It's like a polynomial that never ends,
and it may converge for some x and not

83
00:06:08,195 --> 00:06:08,643
others.

84
00:06:09,766 --> 00:06:15,582
Every power series has a radius of
convergence R. It converges for x within R

85
00:06:15,582 --> 00:06:18,150
of the center and diverges beyond.

86
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:22,035
R can be zero, a positive number, or
infinity.

87
00:06:23,166 --> 00:06:29,478
Use the ratio test. For the sum of x to
the n over n: the ratio is the absolute

88
00:06:29,478 --> 00:06:35,390
value of x times n over n plus one, which
goes to the absolute value of x.

89
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:41,429
It converges when the absolute value of x
is less than one, so R equals one.

90
00:06:42,566 --> 00:06:46,641
At the endpoints, the ratio test fails, so
test them separately.

91
00:06:47,091 --> 00:06:53,429
At x equals one, it's the harmonic series:
diverges. At x equals negative one, it's

92
00:06:53,429 --> 00:06:56,712
the alternating harmonic series:
converges.

93
00:06:57,833 --> 00:07:03,187
So the interval of convergence is from
negative one, included, to one, excluded.

94
00:07:03,637 --> 00:07:06,304
Endpoints can go either way, so always
check.

95
00:07:07,433 --> 00:07:13,470
Your turn. Find the radius of convergence
of the sum of x to the n over n factorial.

96
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:15,456
Pause the video and try it.

97
00:07:15,906 --> 00:07:22,903
The ratio is the absolute value of x over
n plus one, which goes to zero for every

98
00:07:22,903 --> 00:07:24,354
x. R is infinity.

99
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:31,244
In this final lesson of Calculus, a BC
topic, we'll turn Taylor polynomials into

100
00:07:31,244 --> 00:07:34,332
infinite series that equal their
functions.

101
00:07:35,467 --> 00:07:40,565
Let the Taylor polynomial's degree go to
infinity, and you get the Taylor series.

102
00:07:41,015 --> 00:07:45,602
Where the remainder goes to zero, the
series equals the function exactly.

103
00:07:46,733 --> 00:07:52,749
Three series to know by heart: e to the x
is the sum of x to the n over n factorial.

104
00:07:53,199 --> 00:07:59,705
Sine x and cosine x use the odd and even
powers with alternating signs. All three

105
00:07:59,705 --> 00:08:01,391
converge for every x.

106
00:08:02,533 --> 00:08:08,865
Build new series by substitution. e to the
negative x squared is the sum of negative

107
00:08:08,865 --> 00:08:11,579
x squared to the n over n factorial.

108
00:08:12,029 --> 00:08:16,551
That's one minus x squared plus x to the
fourth over two, and so on.

109
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:24,417
Differentiate or integrate term by term.
One over one minus x is one plus x plus x

110
00:08:24,417 --> 00:08:25,892
squared and so on.

111
00:08:26,342 --> 00:08:32,726
Integrating gives negative ln of one minus
x as x plus x squared over two plus x

112
00:08:32,726 --> 00:08:34,961
cubed over three, and so on.

113
